[38;2;124;124;124mdiscrete subgroup. Does G/Γ necessarily have a G-invariant measure?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a classic question about Haar measure and lattices. The answer: G/Γ has [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma G-invariant (Radon) measure if and only if Γ is a lattice, i.e., the quotient [0m[38;2;124;124;124mG/Γ has finite invariant measure (with respect to the measure induced by Haar [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmeasure). But actually the question is whether there exists a G-invariant [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmeasure at all (not necessarily finite).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think carefully. G is locally compact Hausdorff, Γ discrete subgroup. The[0m[38;2;124;124;124mleft Haar measure on G exists. The question is whether it descends to a G-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124minvariant measure on G/Γ.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA G-invariant measure on G/Γ exists iff the modular function of G restricted to [0m[38;2;124;124;124mΓ equals the modular function of Γ (which is trivial since Γ is discrete, so Δ_Γ[0m[38;2;124;124;124m= 1). So we need Δ_G|_Γ = 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, more precisely: Given a left Haar measure μ on G, it descends to a G-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124minvariant measure on G/Γ if and only if Δ_G(γ) = 1 for all γ ∈ Γ, i.e., Γ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontained in the kernel of the modular function.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually let me recall the precise theorem. We have a quotient G/Γ where Γ is a [0m[38;2;124;124;124mclosed subgroup. There's a theorem (Weil's theorem) about when there's an [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minvariant measure on the quotient.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTheorem: Let G be locally compact, H a closed subgroup. There exists a G-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124minvariant Radon measure on G/H (with the quotient being left-invariant under G) [0m[38;2;124;124;124mif and only if Δ_G|_H = Δ_H, where Δ_G is the modular function of G and Δ_H is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe modular function of H.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince Γ is discrete, Δ_Γ ≡ 1. So the condition becomes Δ_G|_Γ ≡ 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is: NOT necessarily. G/Γ has a G-invariant measure iff Γ ⊆ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mker(Δ_G).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo we need a counterexample: a locally compact Hausdorff group G with a discrete[0m[38;2;124;124;124msubgroup Γ such that Δ_G is nontrivial on Γ.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mExample: Take G = the ax+b group (affine group of the real line), which is non-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124munimodular. Its modular function is Δ(a,b) = 1/a (using the parametrization [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(a,b) with a>0, b∈R, action x ↦ ax+b). Actually let me recall: the ax+b group [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwith elements (a,b), a>0, b∈R, multiplication (a,b)(a',b') = (aa', ab'+b). Left [0m[38;2;124;124;124mHaar measure is da db / a^2, right Haar measure is da db / a. The modular [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunction is Δ(a,b) = 1/a.[0m

